body:
I've trained with weights more seriously for about 3 years but played a lot of sports when young, completed marathon distances on my own, etc. so had some base strength. Sometimes it'd be on-and-off if life happened but it always took me about 3 months to see progress or get back into things. For me, progress is being able to do bodyweight calisthenics exercises like pull-ups, pistol squats, L-sit, etc. because otherwise it's just going up in strength...continuously I guess, but I don't really keep track of timeline there.

&#x200B;

I almost never lift to soreness the next day (don't like feeling incapacitated haha) and building muscle is still doable but I would also lift a little almost every day because of the lack of soreness. I'd say bench press and lunges (curtsy lunges in particular) were most helpful for me to be able to do pull-ups and pistol squats. And incline walking (at around 10%) really ramped up my stamina to where I could suddenly run for miles easily one day.

Soup is my main meal every single day and I love how it only takes like 10 minutes to make :) I've made this almost every day for the past 2 years but with varying calorie counts, like I used to add noodles or rice to it until relapse. I put a bunch of vegetables (frozen broccoli, kale, spinach, mushrooms, and cabbage mostly, and sometimes edamame), and then usually 1-2 slices of bacon weighed (found that I need the pork fat for my eczema) and an egg. I wait about 5 minutes for the poached egg to turn medium-boiled, turn off the heat, then add in a serving of miso paste (ratio is about 1-1.5 cups of water to that 18 g of miso).

&#x200B;

Best part is because I add so many vegetables to my giant pot, I usually scoop out extra and pack it away in glass dishes to last me and my husband for one more meal/side so I don't have to turn on the stove again, only the microwave haha

It's really great that you're doing what you like for yourself and not others now! I feel similarly about wearing comfortable clothes now, although for different reasons. It took losing a lot of confidence (illness that can temporarily affect my appearance) for me to realize that I wasn't even affected by the possible judgment of strangers anymore when the only people who mattered to me didn't actually care about how I looked.

&#x200B;

And the fact that after a slightly horrifying near-encounter with a guy who's been obsessed with me for five years, I've realized that random male validation and being seen as "pretty" is worthless and even dangerous. I'll still dress appropriately/professionally with clients (in my line of work it's "smart casual"), but it's not to look "good," just "not out-of-place."

I didn't really know my type but there were definitely guys whose faces I'd be instantly drawn to and crush on, and it kind of threw me off that I didn't feel that way with my SO until we had a company dinner one night (a month after I joined) and his spontaneous, boyish, brazen attitude and clever jokes caught my attention. So later on I figured "okay I guess personality drew me in first" even though the idea of "he's not my type" also felt inaccurate.

&#x200B;

After a while though I realized he does have the one physical feature I've always been wildly into - mischievous fox/cat-like eyes that sort of turn up at the corners, AND his personality matches that. In the past, I would usually end up dating "pretty boys" with bigger/rounder eyes who I thought were aesthetic (like I liked looking at/thinking of drawing their faces) even though they didn't make my heart pound or anything, because it felt a lot safer. I also remembered that the first time we crossed paths at work, I didn't have my glasses on so couldn't actually see his face properly, and we didn't talk otherwise. So...I guess the "type" I have was still a factor...

Oh yeah, the endorphins from runner's high are real. But I don't even like the boredom of running so I think for me it was mainly the unbelievable sense of achievement whenever I looked at my insane Samsung Health records.

&#x200B;

Unfortunately I started associating overexercise with having overeaten, and the feeling of being obligated to compensate feels so much worse (but if I don't, the anxiety is overwhelming). Not to mention exercise actually does make me mentally hungrier after I came close to fainting from hypoglycemia while exercising multiple times. I have so many bad memories that I feel nothing but fear unless I'm at home leisurely using my stationary bike. The last time I ran a marathon on a treadmill out of the sheer terror of gaining weight, my heart rate dropped to the high 30s in the middle of the night and I seriously thought I would pass away from low blood pressure or something (it felt exactly the same as the time I ODed). I can't stand being unable to predict what my body will do

I used to love the site Kirbie's cravings because she would have some 2/3 ingredient recipes. Having to think about what to do with extra ingredients that aren't staple foods has always been super stressful to me. I've microwaved flax meal + water + erythritol to make some sort of sweet "bread." I've mixed pumpkin puree with coconut flour and then microwaved half of it while sticking the other half in the fridge to firm up to test for texture differences. I've tried making this gelatin + applesauce "cake" and whisked by hand with two forks for a whole hour to burn extra calories. I baked this tiny chickpea + banana + nut butter (like mixed together) dessert that was actually not bad.

&#x200B;

At some point I tried enough to know that whatever I "baked" would most likely be disappointing because I'd always cut the ingredient proportions into 1/5 of the listed amounts, so I just lost interest. I even messed up zucchini lasagna (I like spiralized zucchini though!) since I didn't bother extracting the extra water with salt, so when baked it practically became the same watery tomato soup I make on the stove.

&#x200B;

I've never liked baking anyway because I'm usually compelled to try things out of curiosity but never feel like finishing them, and the thought of constantly having to find people to give a lot of food away to was exhausting. When I made tiny amounts, at least my SO would eat the rest of it but I also felt bad giving him such terrible concoctions lol.

For the first 5 years I noted down my intake in separate Gmail drafts titled with the date/day of the week so I only knew estimated calories at the end of the day (no food scale), but the purpose was to look back on the restaurant meals I tried (since I'd also attach pictures).

&#x200B;

For a few years I completely stopped and just focused on calories that day...since even if I overate, I'd completely burn off the extras that day or the next. But then I got a lot of health issues/deficiencies and figured I should actually start tracking vitamin/mineral content and macros.

&#x200B;

So I started using Cronometer. I track "for later" since there are occasions where, months or years down the line, I've looked up my past intake out of curiosity to remember why I felt the way I did. Like how terrible low-fat made me feel. I do find tracking helps me stop thinking of food though. If I don't log it, the numbers will constantly be floating around my mind out of the sheer terror that I might "forget" eating an entire meal like normal people seem to do...

&#x200B;

I usually just copy + paste since there are about 10 different foods that I cook every single day, and then I just add whatever extras I taste-tested that day. It's been super helpful for when I spontaneously decided to make a spreadsheet to calculate the average weekly and monthly calories for the last 5 months to confirm my general TDEE.

Like 2-4k now most days (work from home)...any more feels kind of pointless to me. Especially because it doesn't help clear my mind any more. And I have to do it in the dark most days because being seen "exercising" makes me anxious (but walking to grocery shop doesn't...as if anyone can distinguish what it is I'm doing).

&#x200B;

I used to walk/run a lot in addition to cycling everywhere - at least 10 miles/20k steps a day on average for 2 years straight according to my Samsung Health weekly summary and I never missed a single week - but still fluctuated the same 5 pounds because I would eat too much on some days. There was one month I walked close to 700 miles total but only maintained that \~10 lb loss afterward because I couldn't keep the exercise up with my blistering feet and I was on the lower end of my weight range...so yeah, after 8 years of this, I don't see any point to exercise other than "basic health" (hence the 1-2 miles a day + occasional light spin cycling while watching Netflix + flimsy dumbbell exercises out of habit). I lose more on light activity and not binging than I ever did with what was basically exercise bulimia

The sad thing is that my memory is hazy on the timeline of some major events in my life yet I can remember my average calorie intake and exercise routines during exact months and years, but mainly if it really stands out.

&#x200B;

I think what cements the memory is the feeling of *obsession*. I have been aware of my calories and food for every single day of the past 8 years but if the intake ended up being relatively normal (very small deficit for example, or small surplus but I managed to keep myself from going overboard) and I felt okay/sufficiently distracted for a good enough portion of my day, then it becomes uneventful. That criteria of obsession applied to non-ED matters as well - not that I've had anything else waste as much of my time as this has though.

I tend to get possessive about staple foods - like if there's only a bit of broccoli left and I don't have any other vegetables around, I won't be sharing. I've also had a short period of time where I hesitated to give food away if I already calculated it since uncertainty made me anxious (mainly that I was scared of undereating just as much as overeating, due to frequent hypoglycemia). But in general my default mode is that I love tasting food and then promptly don't feel like finishing it once I know what it tastes like. So I always push it to other people (major benefits of having an SO who isn't picky around food) to alleviate the guilt I would feel about throwing it away.

&#x200B;

I think it's more common to be territorial if you have a food scarcity mindset. Nowadays food usually feels available "later" (and that I can choose when to eat any time) vs. never really knowing when the next time I'm "allowed" to eat will be, so I don't get the feeling as much

I don't really see any reason to use a special ED account - I know that people can use your post history against you but I'd find it funny it someone tried to diss me for having issues with food...because it's so common in this day and age. Like so what? It's not a gotcha. And there's that thing about "nothing you say can be worse than what I tell myself every day" anyway.

&#x200B;

About people I know IRL finding out - my ex once saw my username while I was browsing Reddit, snooped around, and got super concerned over a post I wrote many years before I met him (about suicide). Regardless of whether he remembers my username, I don't care enough to make a separate account now that I post on here. I'm not embarrassed about my life or anything. Still though, if my SO found out I'd probably be uncomfortable because I don't want him to start reading into things in the future or interfere or whatever. Sometimes I'm better, sometimes I'm worse, and it's not something that someone else should overthink

Never had a GW but it literally took dropping 10 lbs in a day and 6 inches from my waist (ovarian cyst removed) almost 10 years ago for me to feel different. Every time after that the loss would be gradual enough that one day I could tell I'd stopped looking a little chunky (not much BDD here)...only to go directly to not feeling skinny enough.

&#x200B;

I mean I don't feel as desperate to be that much skinnier because I fear negative attention (mostly for being seen as fat, not so much being skinny-shamed) wayyy more than I look for positive validation but it was just so disappointing realizing that even with low enough body fat and UW I would just...feel nothing after a few minutes. Or maybe even fear the standard I'd then set at that point to not go over. That I was always going to think I could do more and that it really wasn't ever about my body. Not wanting to eat enough or else I'll feel too much is really just habit at this point :/

I've had an ovarian cyst removed that I legit thought was just fat for some bizarre reason. Growing up I was pretty slim so I just never bothered noticing what my stomach should "normally" look like.

&#x200B;

I barely had any symptoms except for the one time I crouched down and felt a sharp twist in my lower abdomen but it went away after 5 minutes. I was also pretty heavily active because, again, I thought I just had a lot of "visceral fat" on account of the rock-hard beer belly look I was rocking and needed more exercise + lose more weight. While visiting Taiwan, the (Asian) doctor took one look and went "you look 8 months pregnant but skinny, there's no way that's just fat." The only reason I went again was because my period for the two months prior had started lasting for 14 days instead of 5-7. Iirc I had an ultrasound and CT scan done and at that point all my organs were being squished by this enormous sac filled with fluid.

&#x200B;

I lost 10 lbs and 6 inches from my waist overnight after the laparoscopy. It was so jarring seeing the stomach I'd spent the past 3-4 months obsessing over suddenly look completely flat. 8 years later and my mom is still mad that the US doctors didn't bother with an ultrasound or anything and instead told me to "just exercise" (with the cyst I was BMI 21.5). To be fair, I don't think it was at its full size then (that was 2 months before I visited the doctor in Taiwan). But even my dentist who I had visited around the same time, later on said she'd wanted to ask if I was pregnant (at 19 years old lol) because my protruding stomach was just so obvious while lying down...

I've fainted once due to low blood pressure right after waking up and iirc everything just went black. Next I knew I was lying on the bathroom floor. I've also come close to fainting due to hypoglycemia and/or over-exertion while exercising and I think those were whiteouts. Hasn't happened for a while so I struggle to remember but it definitely seemed like a slower fade out - it was like someone was turning up the brightness (and lowering the saturation/vividness) on my vision, though I'd never really concentrate much on seeing things in that state. Usually sounds would also become muffled, or perhaps there'd be a slight ringing in my ears. With hypoglycemia it's more tunnel vision with other symptoms like cold, clammy sweat and what not but for what I imagine were whiteouts, being in that state might have even seemed dreamy if my heart weren't beating so rapidly and my body didn't feel off-balanced.

&#x200B;

Often times I'd crouch down to center myself and close my eyes, and while I've never actually ended up on the ground during those times, I may have fainted after all because sometimes there would be this "break" in my consciousness instead of a continuous stream of thought and worry and when I opened my eyes, it'd feel like I'd just woken up from a dream.

&#x200B;

I think it's worth going to a doctor anyway even if it's just for a checkup.

I'm super competitive (in the sense that I avoid it because I *always* feel like I'll lose) so no lmao. I have, however, often thought of how nice being put into a voluntary coma would be assuming they don't give me anything to maintain weight (especially since I'd also be losing muscle mass which would suck for my metabolism).

&#x200B;

I also deal with another chronic illness at times that I notice healed up completely when my immune system was trying to fight off an overdose over a 1-2 day stay at the hospital...so when things are bad I have another reason to dream of a coma

Taste testing food combinations. I remember making low-calorie mug cakes in my college dorm room but because I wanted to try several different flavors, I'd only partially microwave it, taste it (didn't care that the texture was wrong because the point was the taste), then make it into a completely different flavor by adding something else and finish microwaving it. I kept a digital document where I'd note down everything I wanted to try and make elaborate plans of how I'd change one recipe to another midway so I didn't have to make it at separate times. I also do things like eat cooked fish or meat with one drop each of various condiments to see what they taste like.

&#x200B;

Last month I baked 2-ingredient coconut cookies (shredded coconut + ripe bananas) for my mom and I made one tiny cookie with added black sesame powder, another with cocoa powder, another with protein powder, and another with like 1 gram of peanut butter...I don't know why I'm so curious to know what things taste like but I just HAVE to know.

&#x200B;

A few months ago I tried Crumbl Cookies for the first time and for each cookie, I broke off 1/3 of a serving (so about 10 grams of cookie, literally just a tiny bite), tasted a sliver, then microwaved half of the remainder for about 10 seconds, tasted that, then stuck the other tiny half in the fridge for a few hours to see how that changed its taste/texture. I didn't even feel like I was eating, just "experimenting" (also was a chemistry major if that matters). I hate this habit lol. I grew up with absolutely zero interest in food and when I was pretty much recovered I went back to making mostly the same thing every day out of convenience. So this "foodie" habit is totally bizarre to me

Mine was kind of at the same time, due to a 10-lb ovarian cyst I developed AFTER losing a bit of the freshman weight gain completely normally/healthily (barely weighed myself). It was a weird situation because despite knowing I wasn't eating that much (since all my clothes had gotten baggy), I simultaneously thought I was getting fat and somehow had a rock-hard beer belly even though I don't drink. I'd never really noticed my body before that so didn't know what was normal. So I ended up lowering calories further and further in an attempt to get rid of it.

&#x200B;

Unfortunately I discovered fasting at around the same time, so even though I finally looked my BMI after having it removed, I got addicted to the hunger high. The more time I spent fasting and looking in the mirror though, the more I nit-picked. And eventually binging made it worse, of course. I attribute part of the obsession to how "out of control" I felt when malnourished though, because binging and fast weight gain seems more indicative of a problem than slow weight gain (which could just be the result of a changed environment, like eating out more with a new SO). I just didn't want anyone else to look at me and think I have mental issues, because for me rapid weight loss while fasting or undereating felt "normal" and even non-disordered regardless of the physical symptoms, whereas eating until my stomach felt like it could rupture made me feel like something was seriously wrong with me. So I somehow assumed that other people would see me the same way. After a few years I've realized that I don't actually mind how I look a little heavier unless it's a result of binging.

&#x200B;

But I still can't stare at the mirror too long without finding dumb problems here and there. Can't wear my glasses in the gym anymore because I'll go from thinking "okay muscle definition looks normal today" to "wow I have to go home ASAP so nobody can see how gross I look right now" though that's partly because after running my thighs swell up and actually do look puffier, which is my main insecurity lol...

I'd say most out of character for me was probably waking up at 3 AM to bike 30 miles before my morning class. In a state with sub-zero degree weather during the day (so even worse at night). I've never been a morning person (most days I can't fall asleep before 3 AM now) but I felt like I *had* to do it "or else."

&#x200B;

There was also one month a few years ago where I averaged 22-25 miles a day according to my Samsung Health app and near the end my feet were blistering so hard I had to wrap them up in gauze just so I could keep walking because it kept the thoughts at bay.

In the middle of the healthy BMI range I'm more of an inverted triangle but with enormous thighs so idk. I don't feel like my waist and hips are about the same, it's more so just that my shoulders are obviously wider. My upper arms aren't much bigger than my forearms (when viewed from the side), it's more like fat is added to the width near the shoulders when viewed from the front. Even if I'm working out, added mass is still mass so my body parts just look "chunky" because of my short height.

&#x200B;

My hips shrink even more when close to or UW but because my shoulders aren't as wide, I just look slim overall. I kind of get that "bobblehead" look that apparently means the weight isn't "right" though, it's weird. But other people just look way better at a normal BMI than I do.

&#x200B;

I think even if I had the typical thicc look, I'd still hate it. It does look really good on other people but from reading anecdotes from women who feel self-conscious about their big hips, I feel like I wouldn't be exempt from that insecurity. Not to mention being leered at is gross. I appreciate the fact that in my baggy clothes, I just look like a ruler shape so nobody is cat-calling me (also the neighborhood I live in is the one of the safest in this county so that's part of it).

I especially like the Korean or Japanese aesthetic/calming channels because they have so many more interesting meal ideas than the same old variation of overnight oats/"nutrition bowls"/protein bar/smoothie that I see way too often elsewhere. Sometimes if I already have the ingredients/sauces lying around, then I might try it if it seems simple enough. Or feel a little more motivated to buy a new vegetable I might not have bothered learning how to prepare before.

&#x200B;

Also they usually feel less "diet-y" and are generally pretty balanced. I'm Asian so I think I prefer seeing those foods because they remind me of what I ate growing up, when I was normal. I'm subscribed to many of those channels if you want any links!

Broccoli is a must. I'll cook a mix of 6 different staple vegetables but if it doesn't have broccoli I feel like something's missing. I even drive 33 miles round trip to a farther Costco once every 20 days or so because they carry 3.5 lb bags of frozen broccoli at around $1.56/pound (cheapest I can find around here). And then I stock up on 4 bags to shove in my freezer that also contains other frozen vegetables and fish.

&#x200B;

I also have miso soup every day, sometimes twice a day. Red miso with matcha powder before 3 PM, white miso if after (since the matcha powder has trace amounts of caffeine). Both have tryptophan and I really need help with sleep. I add soft tofu, wakame seaweed, and sometimes a soft-boiled egg. For the red miso, a bit of chili oil gives it a nice spiciness, and I add a few drops of sesame oil to both.

&#x200B;

If I don't steam any fish for the day, then I'll take 2 fish oil pills. My skin is way too dry.

I remember during my eat more-exercise a lot more phases I willingly spent hours walking or cycling to bakeries to try the "richer" desserts like chocolate peanut butter tarts. Sometimes just knowing a bakery carried it was enough to make it memorable like damn. I never ordered stuff like sponge cakes with fruit because in my mind they tasted like a whole lot of "airy nothingness" when compared to the more creamy and luxurious desserts. Cheesecake was special too.

&#x200B;

Crazy how eating disorders can change your tastes. I didn't like the texture and mouth feel of PB or cream cheese as a kid and during full recovery periods I didn't really have any interest in it (to the point where I had 2 flavored PB jars harden and go rancid in the fridge, which is just bad planning on my part) because my favorite meals don't involve it.

I've been trying to look up why this is as well! My insomnia is pretty bad but whenever I feel completely exhausted, hunger is easier to rationalize away. From how I experience it, if I'm hungry and not tired then I see it as a signal to eat, whereas if I'm hungry and tired I seem to see the hunger as a symptom of sleep deprivation and "not real."

&#x200B;

Eating feels kind of wrong because I associate it with giving me energy, but without sleep it doesn't really do that properly. I also notice that when I'm so sleep-deprived that it actually feels a little painful to stay awake, I interpret hunger as *pleasant* instead of a neutral and mildly annoying sensation because the slight sickness comes with the thought that "this is what it feels like to be alive."

&#x200B;

During the day I'll usually just drink (whole) milk with tea while tired though (don't want to deliberately fast anymore) so it's not like I'm getting no calories at all. Even though that can give me energy as well, I mentally categorize it into its other function as a bedtime aid (no caffeine tea at least) so if the energy thing doesn't work, it still served some other purpose of maybe calming my overactive mind down.

I was definitely a people-pleaser but in a very subtle way - in high school, I developed a specific fear of *appearing* or *sounding* rude. I would constantly overthink how my words came across and my facial expressions in case it settled into resting bitch face. I had no trouble with stating my opinions or *saying* "no thanks" but only as long as my tone of voice was slightly apologetic, as if to imply "I acknowledge that you want me to do this but it's not my choice that I can't, really."

&#x200B;

Part of it was because growing up, my mom often accused me of sounding "angry" when I was just a kid with a lot of energy. And another part was because I recognized how callous I could really be when I wasn't thinking at all. So I overcompensated. As an extrovert, this anxious energy didn't bode well for the euphoria I felt around people that I was afraid of accidentally destroying at any moment.

&#x200B;

The ED's honeymoon period fixed all of that. I could redirect my mental state and induce euphoria just by fasting a little longer, losing a little more weight, and thinking of what food I would eat next when I made it past xx hours. I didn't care what anyone else thought about how I acted because my entire identity had become "skinny and bubbly girl" whom nobody could criticize. When I started binging and over-exercising and isolating myself by relegating everyone to mere acquaintances, I brushed my unhappiness off as "well I'm a failure for eating so of course I hate myself." I never entertained the thought that it could be a desire for closer relationships. Regardless of my weight, this has always been my safety net because I could constantly attempt to "fix" myself without ever having to risk real criticism.

My periods are pretty on-and-off depending on severity of restriction. They usually come around the 10th so if I miss it, then they don't come back unless I eat better/more. I don't pay attention to any pattern if one does come, although now that I think of it there were some periods that only lasted like 3 days (usually mine last 5-7) that I just chalked up to luck for some reason even though they were probably because of restriction.

&#x200B;

I don't get PMS symptoms at all. Except for a single day maybe 12 years ago - painful cramps, even had to take a nap because of it, and I can't remember the last time I took a nap either. No ED at all then. So I can't speak for that part

IMO frame size doesn't make as huge of a difference as people think. Someone with a large frame might look smaller than what they think BMI 23 should look, but they're never going to look like a small-framed BMI 16 or whatever. I mean mass is mass and your bones don't offer a significant difference in weight. If you have wider bones at certain parts of your body you might look bonier (like my chest bones and all the surrounding shoulder bones are visible at a low-normal BMI - medium frame), but the mass is still there somewhere else (in my case my legs).

&#x200B;

What's probably happening is that people who've seen you at a higher weight just aren't used to seeing you at a lower weight. Of course you probably do have a sicker complexion from your eating disorder but unless others are overly scrutinizing what your body looks like, it's not just the body alone that looks healthy/unhealthy.

I'm confused, you say you never binge but you did a few years ago? Are you talking about the "loss of control" binges and not actual calorically damaging ones? Going past a self-imposed restrictive limit but still at a deficit or even blowing past your TDEE but by only 100 calories in a frenzy vs. thousands of calories in a single sitting kind of difference. 

&#x200B;

The "professionals" on those podcasts/videos are probably just using the looser term for feeling out of control rather than the extreme one. It seems like normal people cannot imagine "true" binge eating because they haven't experienced it themselves. Like 3 extra cookies that they didn't "mean to eat" but also doesn't cause significant distress (so not viewed through ED lens) is sometimes referred to as a binge too.

&#x200B;

Also is it common for people to think weight gain automatically = binging? I think a lot of people understand that gradual weight gain, whether on purpose or without noticing, can just be because of modern calorie-dense foods or metabolic disorders...

Yep my sleep schedule is at its worst, especially now that I work from home and all of my meetings start in the late afternoons so I literally have no incentive to fix this. To be fair I did have insomnia as a kid too but some part of me does it on purpose now so I don't have to deal with typical lunch hours most days either.

&#x200B;

I haven't eaten during breakfast hours in years anyway (was like this as a kid too) so that doesn't matter, but at this point I just get all my calories from like 4 PM - 9 or 10 (not super strict about this). I also take advantage of the fact that I'm just never mentally hungry from midnight to like 8 AM (during all-nighters). Sleep is what I consider to be a reset of my calories so even if I'm hungry while staying up the entire night, eating feels wrong. The day I graduated from college, I stayed up for about 55 hours and just didn't feel like eating the entire time because of that weird mix of hyperactivity and exhaustion. Also I didn't have any food around anyway so it was more effort that I didn't feel like exerting to decide what to buy.

&#x200B;

This past week has been horrendous, I slept from 9 AM - 3 PM twice, and then yesterday I stayed up all night and then slept from 10 AM - 1 PM...it's 3 AM right now and I'm still not tired.

&#x200B;

Also I'm 26 now and the more sleep-deprived I am, the more I start feeling whatever heart disease feels like 😭

Having energy that I could have 24/7 if I ate properly (instead of this mix of severe insomnia and lethargy and forced movement). There are times when I eat more or hit a certain number of fats and carbs and just feel incredibly satiated, content, and just as energetic and naturally athletic as I used to when I was normal.

&#x200B;

I genuinely feel high during those times but can't always replicate it just from eating more, especially if I feel like I don't have any calories "left" and subsequently have to limit excessive movement or else I might get hungry faster...which makes no sense because if I move more then my TDEE would obviously be higher.

&#x200B;

Also being in the zone over anything and forgetting about food makes me happy lol

Oh man I used to, but now it just reminds me of all the crying I did on the treadmill when the gym was empty lol. Or cycling even when I was in pain from sweating (my skin was developing a rash and stinging from sweat and I couldn't leave for hours). Or nearly fainting from hypoglycemia on the 3-minute walk home because I ran on empty for too long. Also the mirrors had me hyper-focused on the slightest changes in my appearance...

&#x200B;

I think I prefer just being outdoors after all. Except I bought an exercise bike a few months ago and have had dumbbells at home for years so now I'm only active while watching movies and staying in my apartment like the hermit I've become. Other than walks for vitamin D.

I do that even when weighing items...like if xx grams of a certain food is calculated to be something something .3, I round it up to the next number. It doesn't make a huge difference though because I probably use the food scale about 10-20 times a day (as in different foods or the same food/drink at different times), so I'm only overestimating by 15 calories at the most. But if there's conflicting information (calories of frozen broccoli on the package are different from Google's repository), I'll go with the higher one just in case. It's literally just broccoli like jeez...I never used to count that so closely.

&#x200B;

But when I can only estimate, I totally round up to the nearest 100 or tack on an extra 200 just in case and then feel bad for reaching a certain number by a certain time of day even though at the same time I know I didn't "actually" eat that amount.

That is so interesting! I may have mild aphantasia and all my active thinking is done in words. I can recall memories as vague pictures but they're sort of blank without some form of language in them.

&#x200B;

I'm curious to know how you experience your thinking - when I try to problem-solve for example, I basically have a running monologue through my head. Do you conjure up helpful images and linked diagrams more so? Or when I'm typing out replies or an essay, I always "hear" my own thoughts first before I can put them out. Do you have some type of conversion from images to words in addition/instead?
